4. Severity-classify every finding as exactly BLOCKER / HIGH / MEDIUM / LOW.
   Evaluate likelihood, reachability, reversibility, and concrete consequence
   against the declared assurance context above. Do not turn a spec mismatch
   into a BLOCKER mechanically: when repository evidence shows the contract is
   wrong, identify that evidence and the needed contract correction so the
   remediation seat can propose a structured amendment instead of guessing.
   BLOCKER: consequential spec violation; data loss or leak; security hole (injection, auth
   bypass, secrets exposure, path traversal) even if unlikely; crash on normal
   input; backward-compatibility break without intent; CI failing because of
   this PR.
   HIGH: correctness bug no criterion caught; missing validation with no known
   exploit; a race on a path that will hit it; resource leak; test gap on a
   critical path; >10% regression on a hot path; an error swallowed silently.
   MEDIUM: code that will rot, misleading naming, one missing edge-case test,
   doc drift, convention mismatch. Does not block approval.
   LOW: nits. Never load-bearing.
   NOT findings: style with no concrete problem, hypothetical refactors,
   unmeasured "could be faster", fan-fic about unrealistic inputs.
5. Verdict — classify every finding first, then decide. Any BLOCKER: localized
   to a line or two → request-changes; spread across the diff → block. No
   BLOCKER but a HIGH → request-changes. Neither, all criteria met, CI green,
   and you read every changed file → approve. When torn between block and
   request-changes, prefer request-changes and say the gap is wide.

